 pair be (a,b) with a < b, so difference = a-b = negative, abs = b-a. Let there be another pair (c,d) with c > d, difference = c-d positive, and c-d > b-a. Then Q1 gives b-a, Q2 gives c-d, and they differ since b-a < c-d. For instance, points at X = 1, 3, 5. Differences: 1-3=-2 (abs=2), 1-5=-4 (abs=4), 3-5=-2 (abs=2), 3-1=2, 5-1=4, 5-3=2. Q1: MIN(ABS) = 2. Q2: MIN(A.X-B.X) where A.X>B.X: possibilities: 3-1=2, 5-1=4, 5-3=2, so MIN=2. Same. To differ, we need the smallest absolute difference to be unique to a negative difference. Add point 0. Points: 0, 1, 3, 5. Compute all differences: 0-1=-1 (abs=1), 0-3=-3 (abs=3), 0-5=-5 (abs=5), 1-3=-2 (abs=2), 1-5=-4 (abs=4), 3-5=-2 (abs=2), and positives: 1-0=1, 3-0=3, 5-0=5, 3-1=2, 5-1=4, 5-3=2. Q1: MIN(ABS)=1 (from 0-1 or 1-0). Q2: MIN(positive)=1 (from 1-0). Same. Need where the smallest abs is from a negative and there is no positive of that magnitude. That means the closest pair must have a < b, and no pair has b-a as a positive difference. But since it's a cross join, if (a,b) gives negative difference d=a-b, then (b,a) gives positive difference b-a = -d. So if there is a negative difference, its absolute value appears as a positive difference from the reversed pair. Therefore, MIN(ABS) will always equal MIN(positive difference) because for every pair (x,y) with x≠y, ABS(x-y) = ABS(y-x), and one of x-y or y-x is positive if x≠y. Actually, if x>y, then x-y positive, ABS=x-y. If x<y, then y-x positive, ABS=y-x. So the set of absolute differences is exactly the set of positive differences from pairs where A.X > B.X. Thus Q1 and Q2 should be equivalent? Let's check: Q1: MIN(ABS(A.X - B.X)) over all pairs with A.X ≠ B.X. Q2: MIN(A.X - B.X) over all pairs with A.X > B.X. For any pair (a,b) with a≠b, let d = ABS(a-b). There exists an ordered pair (max(a,b), min(a,b)) where max(a,b) > min(a,b) and max(a,b) - min(a,b) = d. So the set of values considered by Q2 is exactly the set of absolute differences from distinct pairs. Therefo