ey are equivalent if the HAVING conditions are the same. But wait, in Q2, the GROUP BY is on PRODUCT_ID, and HAVING uses MIN(S.SALE_DATE) and MAX(S.SALE_DATE). If a product has no sales, it won't appear in the result because the JOIN will produce no rows, so no group is formed. If a product has sales, the MIN and MAX are computed over all sales for that product. In Q1, the subquery computes MIN and MAX over all sales for each product and filters. So they are semantically equivalent. However, there is a difference: Q1 uses an INNER JOIN between PRODUCT and the subquery, so if a product has no rows in the subquery (i.e., no sales meeting the date condition), it's excluded. Q2 uses a JOIN (implicitly INNER JOIN) between PRODUCT and SALES, so if a product has no sales at all, it's excluded. But if a product has sales but none meet the date condition, in Q1, it's excluded (since subquery has no rows for it), and in Q2, it's also excluded (because the JOIN produces rows, but the HAVING filters them out). So still equivalent. To break equivalence, we need a case where a product has sales that meet the date condition, but additional rows in the join cause the HAVING in Q2 to fail. This can't happen because the MIN and MAX are over all sales rows. Let's construct a minimal example: Product A has one sale on '2019-02-01'. Both queries include it. Product B has two sales: one on '2019-02-01' and one on '2019-04-01'. Q1: subquery groups by PRODUCT_ID for B, MIN='2019-02-01', MAX='2019-04-01', HAVING fails because MAX > '2019-03-31', so B excluded. Q2: join all sales for B, group by PRODUCT_ID, MIN='2019-02-01', MAX='2019-04-01', HAVING fails, so B excluded. Same. So no difference. But what if a product has sales only within the range, but the join in Q2 includes duplicate rows that don't affect MIN/MAX? Still same. I realize the error: in Q2, the GROUP BY is on PRODUCT_ID, but the SELECT includes P.PRODUCT_NAME without aggregating it. This is invalid in standard SQL if PRODUCT_