{
    "explanation": "Q1 calculates the minimum absolute difference between all distinct pairs of X values (excluding self-pairs), while Q2 calculates the minimum absolute difference between consecutive X values when sorted ascending. Q2 misses non-consecutive pairs that might have smaller differences than any consecutive pair.",
    "counterexample_dsp": "Three points where the smallest difference is between non-consecutive values: points at X=1, X=3, and X=4. The consecutive differences are |3-1|=2 and |4-3|=1, so Q2 returns 1. However, the non-consecutive pair (1,4) has difference 3, and all other non-consecutive pairs have larger differences, so Q1 also considers the pair (1,3) with difference 2, but the minimum is still 1 from (3,4). Wait, that doesn't produce different results. Let's adjust: points at X=1, X=4, X=5. Consecutive differences: |4-1|=3, |5-4|=1 → Q2 returns 1. Non-consecutive pairs: (1,5) diff=4, so Q1 considers (1,4) diff=3 and (4,5) diff=1 → Q1 also returns 1. Need a case where the smallest difference is between non-consecutive points. Try points at X=1, X=3, X=4, X=6. Consecutive diffs: 2,1,2 → Q2 returns 1 (from 3,4). Non-consecutive pairs include (4,6) diff=2, (1,3) diff=2, (1,4) diff=3, (3,6) diff=3, (1,6) diff=5 → Q1 also returns 1. Actually, the consecutive pair always has the smallest difference if points are sorted? Not necessarily: consider points at X=1, X=4, X=5, X=8. Consecutive diffs: 3,1,3 → Q2 returns 1. Non-consecutive: (4,5) diff=1 is consecutive, so Q1 also returns 1. To make Q2 miss a smaller difference, we need a gap between consecutive points larger than a non-consecutive difference. Example: points at X=1, X=10, X=11. Consecutive diffs: 9 and 1 → Q2 returns 1. Non-consecutive: (1,11) diff=10, so Q1 also returns 1. Need at least 4 points: X=1, X=4, X=5, X=7. Consecutive diffs: 3,1,2 → Q2 returns 1. Non-consecutive pairs: (1,4)=3, (1,5)=4, (1,7)=6, (4,5)=1 (consecutive), (4,7)=3, (5,7)=2. So Q1 also returns 1. The issue is that for any set, the minimum difference between any two points must occur between some consecutive points when sorted, because if points a<b<c and |a-c| is minimal, then |a-b| or |b-c| is ≤ |a-c|. Proof: |a-c| = |a-b|+|b-c| ≥ min(|a-b|,|b-c|). So Q2 should always return the same as Q1. But wait, Q1 excludes pairs where A.X = B.X, b